sa.py","description":"Run the VISA open/close separator under bgrun","run_in_background":true}},{"tool_name":"Bash","tool_use_id":"toolu_01RBAyMTPPeHnv75Rh3aNSre","tool_input":{"command":"cd \"G:/Codes/LabVIEW_Codes/MinLab/zz_LabView VI/AAA_UNIST/2. Tracking/V6_ParallelLoop\" && py tools/bgrun.py --max-min 10 --log tools/bench/retro.log -- py tools/retrospective.py --cycle 105","timeout":660000,"description":"Run the cycle-105 retrospective under bgrun"}},{"tool_name":"Monitor","tool_use_id":"toolu_01Ka7wJZwiDFQKif718reLzV","tool_input":{"description":"cycle-105 retrospective BGRUN END/TIMEOUT","timeout_ms":720000,"command":"cd \"G:/Codes/LabVIEW_Codes/MinLab/zz_LabView VI/AAA_UNIST/2. Tracking/V6_ParallelLoop\"; until grep -qE \"BGRUN (END|TIMEOUT|KILLED)\" tools/bench/retro.log 2>/dev/null && tail -n 3 tools/bench/retro.log | grep -qE \"BGRUN (END|TIMEOUT|KILLED)\"; do sleep 5; done; grep -E \"BGRUN (END|TIMEOUT|KILLED)|VIOLATION|archive/peer\" tools/bench/retro.log | tail -n 8"}},{"tool_name":"Bash","tool_use_id":"toolu_01FqBGK456sZQqTXPQCJDKtr","tool_input":{"command":"f=\"C:/Users/KimLab/AppData/Local/Temp/claude/G--Codes-LabVIEW-Codes-MinLab-zz-LabView-VI-AAA-UNIST-2--Tracking-V6-ParallelLoop/9960f7ab-99c2-447e-bae7-c800e88479c4/tasks/b1uwlll5p.output\"; for i in $(seq 1 55); do grep -qE \"BGRUN (END|TIMEOUT|KILLED)\" \"$f\" && break; sleep 5; done; tail -n 4 \"$f\"","timeout":300000,"description":"Wait up to ~5 min for the retrospective to end"}}],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":4,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":4,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":1,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":4,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{"material":4}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":68,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"Cycle 105 is done. I found why every real run of the VI has stopped since yesterday: NI-VISA, the driver layer LabVIEW uses for serial ports, refuses the rotor's port (COM5), even with LabVIEW closed. It needs your action before the benchmark can run.\n\n**What each diagnostic step found:**\n- **The error (105-1, 105-2):** 0.41 s after Run, the unchanged copy of the original VI stops on an error window: *\"Error -1073807246 at VISA Open in Configure.vi — the resource is valid, but VISA cannot currently access it.\"* Another window was covering it on screen, so I added a small read-only option to `tools/lv_gui.ps1` that captures a window by its handle.\n- **COM5 at the Windows level (105-3):** Windows sees COM5 as free while the error window is up. The Windows event log shows no USB device plugged, unplugged or reconfigured since 09-26 12:00.\n- **The deciding check (105-4, no LabVIEW running):** VISA refuses both `Rotor` and `ASRL5::INSTR` (3 of 3 tries each). Windows opens COM5 normally, and VISA opens the other USB-serial port, COM6, 3 of 3.\n- **What this rules out:** neither the copy of the original nor the new display-loop VI causes it. I did not change any VI and did not point the rotor at a different port.\n\n**What is not known:** when this started, and whether our own forced shutdowns of LabVIEW while it held COM5 open caused it.\n\n**Your decision (D-2026-09-27-03):** clearing it needs one of these:\n- you restart the PC (the runner must be stopped first);\n- you unplug and replug the rotor's USB-serial adapter (FTDI, serial AI03Q9JZA);\n- we restart NI's Windows services by script at a cycle boundary.\n\nI recommend the replug if you are near the rig, otherwise a PC restart. A service restart is less certain to work, and it also touches the camera and motor drivers. D-2026-09-27-01 and -02 are still open too; -02 (did anything change on the rig?) is partly answered, since no device change was recorded.\n\n**Next cycle (106):** it first repeats the 12-second VISA check. If the port opens, the before/after frame-loss benchmark from plan item 217(f) runs unchanged. If not, the next cycle does the pending tooling repairs instead.\n\n**Retrospective:** it found one fault, and it was mine. The third diagnostic step assumed another program was holding the port, when the 12-second VISA-only check could have come first; that cost about 28 minutes. I accepted it and added a rule to plan item 218(e): when an error names a driver layer, open the resource through that layer outside LabVIEW before any LabVIEW run. I also accepted two changes for the tooling repairs:\n- a diagnostic run closes LabVIEW immediately after its last screenshot, instead of waiting about 4 minutes for a failed stop;\n- every run first checks that the rotor port opens through VISA.\n\n`STATUS.md` NEXT, `tools/bench/next.json` and plan item 218 are updated. The Claude Figma connector needs authorising in your claude.ai connector settings if you want it; this cycle did not use it.","ttft_ms":2167,"type":"result","duration_ms":5353215,"uuid":"df704f0e-4bac-4cc4-a217-ffacc3c9d0e2","ttft_stream_ms":2011,"time_to_request_ms":240,"first_content_frame_ms":2011,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
